        "reported_material_gaps": [],
        "research_rationale": "Executive order approving the Code of Fair Competition for the Builders Supplies Trade under section 3 of Title I of the National Industrial Recovery Act. Applying the reviewed shared analysis: the code-dependent command had no own-terms end, no held record shows an earlier cancellation or suspension, and Senate Joint Resolution 113 section 2 (June 14, 1935) repealed the code approval and enforcement provisions, so the approval ended on June 14, 1935. Schechter (May 27, 1935) is a validity characterization, not an end date. The text is an American Presidency Project transcription held as a provisional working text under the 2026-10-21 owner ruling; no certification is claimed.\nMember checks: {\"earlier_end_check\":\"The order states no period, condition, \\\"until\\\" date, pending clause or provisional approval, and the lookup found no own-text signal. The held-corpus search found no cancellation, modification under section 10(b), superseding code, revocation or suspension of this code before June 14, 1935. A held later order, Executive Order 6885 of October 25, 1934, approves an amended code for the Builders Supplies Trade and states \\\"that the previous approval of said Code is hereby modified to include an approval of said Code in its entirety as amended\\\"; it amends and continues the approval rather than revoking, cancelling or replacing the code, so it is a later event, not an end. No held record shows this code cancelled or suspended before June 14, 1935, so the first end is the statutory repeal; June 16, 1935 is not an end because section 1 of S.J. Res. 113 replaced that expiry with April 1, 1936.\",\"independent_command_check\":\"The order contains no appointment, committee, delegation, report direction or other command independent of the code; its other words are the recital of the application, hearings and the Administrator's findings, the signature and the Administrator's recommendation. \",\"savings_check\":\"Acts done and liabilities incurred under the Code of Fair Competition for the Builders Supplies Trade before June 14, 1935, and any fine, injunction or conviction already completed, are accrued effects that the repeal did not undo; the section 5 antitrust exemption for action already taken under an approved and effective code is likewise an accrued effect. The proviso to section 2 of the resolution names agreements and action thereunder and states no time limit, and the held text does not settle whether it narrowed that exemption for past action under codes. No held source continues the approval after June 14, 1935 and no held source shows a prosecution under this code continued after that date; nothing here continues the approval itself.\",\"scope_check\":\"Identity: the census register lists this order as 1933-10-03, Franklin D. Roosevelt; the WPA 1944 list has a row for the same date, page 541, stating the code as approved; the American Presidency Project page record for the held text's sha256 is a common-crawl capture whose heading names the order's number, whose date is 1933-10-03 and whose text_sha256 equals the held text's, so the page check passes. The inventory is one clause, the sole operative paragraph that approves the code, which is complete; the held text was read in full. \"}",
